{"result":"{
  "metadata": {
    "id": "450068481",
    "title": "04.4.5 Product Images — View Types & MuleSoft Mapping Rules",
    "type": "page",
    "created": "",
    "updated": "",
    "url": "https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/SFCC/pages/450068481",
    "space": {
      "key": "SFCC",
      "name": "Adobe SFCC"
    },
    "version": 4,
    "attachments": [],
    "content": {
      "value": "Overview\n--------\n\nProduct images are ingested into SFCC via MuleSoft as part of the product feed. The studio team uploads images to a shared folder structure, which IT is connecting to MuleSoft. MuleSoft then delivers images into SFCC alongside the product data feed.\n\nThis page documents: - How images are organised in SFCC using **View Types** - The **naming convention rules** MuleSoft must apply to assign images to the correct view type - How **alt text** is derived from view types - An open item around **accessories vs. clothing** category split\n\n---\n\nImage Ingestion Flow\n--------------------\n\nStudio uploads images → Shared folder → MuleSoft → SFCC (via product feed alongside AP21 data)\n\nMuleSoft is responsible for:\n\n1. Reading the image file from the shared folder\n2. Applying naming convention logic to determine the correct SFCC view type\n3. Delivering the image to SFCC assigned to the correct view type and product\n\n---\n\nSFCC View Types\n---------------\n\nView types in SFCC are used to make an image relevant for a specific placement on the website. The PWA storefront dynamically renders the appropriate image size and aspect ratio — the out-of-the-box SFCC size buckets (Large, Medium, Small) are **not used** for rendering purposes.\n\nThe view type `large` is used as the **primary/canonical sync folder** — this is where all images are synced first and where the gallery display order is determined. All other view types represent specific placements or image roles.\n\n| View Type | Purpose | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `large` | Primary sync folder, gallery order source of truth | All images synced here first |\n| `flat_item` | Flat/ghost mannequin product shot | PNG required for dynamic background colour treatment |\n| `detail` | Close-up detail image | Maps to tag: *Detail Image* |\n| `model` | On-model image | Maps to tag: *Model Image*; used as hero/rollover on PLP hover state |\n| `front_view` | Front-facing model image | Typically image position 3? TBD |\n| `side_view` | Side-facing model image | Typically image position 4? TBD |\n| `back_view` | Back-facing model image | Typically image position 5? TBD |\n| `product_styling` | Product styled/lifestyle image | Used in the smaller Shop the Look component |\n| `campaign` | Campaign imagery | Used in the larger Shop the Look component; appears at end of PDP carousel |\n| `runway` | Runway imagery | Used in the larger Shop the Look component; appears at end of PDP carousel |\n| `hidden` | Hidden from PDP gallery display | Used to suppress images — can be model images used for rollover only |\n\n> **Reference:** View types are formally defined in [SFCC-197](https://zimmermannworkboard.atlassian.net/browse/SFCC-197).\n\n---\n\nMuleSoft Naming Convention Rules\n--------------------------------\n\nMuleSoft must apply logic at the point of image ingestion to determine which view type to assign each image. This logic will be based on **image file naming conventions** established by the studio team.\n\n### Rules to Define in MuleSoft Workshop\n\nThe following rules need to be confirmed and configured with the MuleSoft team:\n\n| Naming Convention Pattern | Assigned View Type | Notes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| TBD | `large` | Default/all images |\n| TBD | `flat_item` | — |\n| TBD | `detail` | — |\n| TBD | `model` | — |\n| TBD | `front_view` | Confirm which image number (expected: position 3) |\n| TBD | `side_view` | Confirm which image number (expected: position 4) |\n| TBD | `back_view` | Confirm which image number (expected: position 5) |\n| TBD | `campaign` | — |\n| TBD | `runway` | — |\n| TBD | `hidden` | — |\n\n> ⚠️ **Action required:** Naming conventions need to be confirmed with the studio team before the MuleSoft workshop. MuleSoft will need these rules documented to build the mapping logic.\n\n---\n\nAlt Text Derivation\n-------------------\n\nAlt text on product images is generated automatically based on the view type tag associated with the image, combined with the product name. This avoids the need for manual alt text entry in Business Manager (which is not part of the content team's workload).\n\n### Logic\n\n* **If an image has a view type tag:** Alt text = `[View Type Label] of [Product Name]`\n* **If an image has no tag:** Alt text = `[Product Name]`\n\n### Examples\n\n| Product Name | View Type | Generated Alt Text |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | Detail Image | Detail image of Valiant Batwing Gown |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | Model Image | Model image of Valiant Batwing Gown |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | Front View | Front view of Valiant Batwing Gown |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | Campaign | Campaign image of Valiant Batwing Gown |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | Runway | Runway image of Valiant Batwing Gown |\n| Valiant Batwing Gown | *(none)* | Valiant Batwing Gown |\n\n> **Note:** Alt text can be manually overridden per image in SFCC Business Manager if required, though this is not expected to be part of regular operations.\n\n---\n\nOpen Item: Accessories vs. Clothing Split\n-----------------------------------------\n\nThere is an open question around whether the front/side/back view type logic (position-based image numbering) should apply differently to **accessories** vs **clothing**.\n\n**Background:** The position-based mapping (e.g. image 3 = front view, image 4 = side view, image 5 = back view) is likely only valid for **clothing categories**. Accessories do not follow the same image series conventions and would not map reliably to these view types.\n\n**Hypothesis:** MuleSoft should be able to apply category-aware logic since product category data is available as part of the AP21 product feed — meaning MuleSoft would know whether an image belongs to a clothing or accessories product at the time of ingestion.\n\n> ⚠️ **To be confirmed with MuleSoft team:** Whether category-level conditional logic is feasible within the MuleSoft integration. This cannot be confirmed until the MuleSoft team is engaged and the feed structure is reviewed.\n\n---\n\nMuleSoft Workshop — Items to Align\n----------------------------------\n\nThe following items must be resolved during the MuleSoft onboarding workshop:\n\n1. Confirm image file naming conventions with studio team\n2. Map naming conventions to SFCC view types (see table above), including Campaign and Runway\n3. Confirm image position numbers for front/side/back view types (clothing)\n4. Confirm whether category-aware logic is feasible for accessories split\n5. Confirm MuleSoft can access product category as part of the feed at image ingestion time\n\n---\n\n*Page owner: Jasmijn | Last updated: September 2026*",
